    {
      "id": "kafka-consumer-groups-explained",
      "heading": "Kafka Consumer Groups – Explained",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A Consumer Group is a set of consumers that work together to consume data from a topic in parallel."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Behavior:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Each partition is consumed by only one consumer in a group.",
            "Kafka ensures load balancing."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Example:"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "If a topic has 3 partitions and 3 consumers in a group, each consumer gets 1 partition."
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 53,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "how-kafka-tracks-offsets",
      "heading": "How Kafka Tracks Offsets",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Kafka stores offsets for each consumer group:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Stored in a special internal topic: __consumer_offsets",
            "Offset = the position of the last message that was consumed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Two types of offset management:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Automatic (default): Kafka commits offsets periodically",
            "Manual: User commits offsets (e.g., using commitAsync())"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 43,
      "subsections": []
    },
